Package: lsastrat
Title: Stratification and Inequality Metrics for Large-Scale Assessment Data
Version: 0.3.0
Authors@R:
    person("Kevin", "Schoenholzer", , "kevinschonholzer@gmail.com",
           role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"),
           comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-9892-5869"))
Description: Computes educational stratification and inequality quantities
    from large-scale assessment (LSA) data such as PISA, TIMSS and PIRLS as
    first-class outputs, with plausible-value pooling (Rubin's rules) and
    survey replicate-weight variance estimation (balanced repeated
    replication, including Fay's method, and jackknife). Provides the
    socio-economic achievement gradient (slope, strength and curvature),
    school-level segregation indices with a clustered bootstrap and
    finite-sample bias correction, and the Ferreira-Gignoux decomposition of
    inequality of educational opportunity. Further tools decompose these
    quantities (between/within-school variance, a Shapley decomposition of
    inequality of opportunity, and Oaxaca-Blinder achievement-gap
    decomposition), compare them across groups, and run plausible-value and
    replicate-weight aware specification-curve (multiverse) analyses.
    Complements general LSA packages (such as 'EdSurvey', 'intsvy' and
    'BIFIEsurvey') by packaging stratification measures that those tools leave
    to be assembled by hand. Methods follow Rubin (1987)
    <doi:10.1002/9780470316696> for plausible-value pooling, Ferreira and
    Gignoux (2014) <doi:10.1093/wber/lht004> for inequality of educational
    opportunity, and Elbers (2023) <doi:10.1177/0049124121986204> for
    finite-sample segregation bias correction.
